,"civil engineer"],"wiki":"Manuel Serifo Nhamadjo"},"Q627337":{"id":"Q627337","name":"Rui Duarte de Barros","desc":"Guinea-Bissauan prime minister","img":null,"gender":"M","born":"1960-02-18","died":null,"birthplace":"Guinea-Bissau","parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Rui Duarte de Barros"},"Q16908893":{"id":"Q16908893","name":"José Mário Vaz","desc":"President of Guinea-Bissau from 2014 to 2020","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Jos%C3%A9_M%C3%A1rio_Vaz_on_March_25%2C_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Jos%C3%A9_M%C3%A1rio_Vaz_on_March_25%2C_2025_%28cropped%29.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1957-12-10","died":null,"birthplace":"Cacheu","parties":["African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde"],"education":[],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"José Mário Vaz"},"Q16736700":{"id":"Q16736700","name":"Domingos Simoes Pereira","desc":"Prime Minister of Guinea-Bissau","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Domingos_Sim%C3%B5es_Pereira.jpg/330px-Domingos_Sim%C3%B5es_Pereira.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1963-10-20","died":null,"birthplace":"Farim","parties":["African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde"],"education":["Odesa Polytechnic National University","California State University, Fresno","Catholic University of Portugal"],"occupations":["politician","civil engineer","minister"],"wiki":"Domingos Simões Pereira"},"Q4838987":{"id":"Q4838987","name":"Baciro Djá","desc":"Guinea-Bissauan politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Baciro_Dj%C3%A1.png/330px-Baciro_Dj%C3%A1.png","gender":"M","born":"1973-01-31","died":null,"birthplace":"Guinea-Bissau","parties":["African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde"],"education":["University of Havana"],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Baciro Djá"},"Q16855164":{"id":"Q16855164","name":"Umaro Sissoco Embaló","desc":"President of Guinea-Bissau from 2020 to 2025","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Umaro_Sissoco_Embal%C3%B3_on_February_11%2C_2025.jpg/330px-Umaro_Sissoco_Embal%C3%B3_on_February_11%2C_2025.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1972-09-23","died":null,"birthplace":"Bissau","parties":["African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde","Madem G-15"],"education":["Centro Superior de Estudios de la Defensa Nacional"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Umaro Sissoco Embaló"},"Q47527935":{"id":"Q47527935","name":"Artur Silva","desc":"politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Artur_Silva_novo_PM_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Artur_Silva_novo_PM_%28cropped%29.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1956-00-00","died":null,"birthplace":"Bissau","parties":["African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde"],"education":["University of Hull"],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Artur Silva"},"Q86715481":{"id":"Q86715481","name":"Nuno Nabiam","desc":"Prime Minister of Guinea-Bissau","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Nuno_Gomes_Nabiam.png/330px-Nuno_Gomes_Nabiam.png","gender":"M","born":"1966-11-17","died":null,"birthplace":"Bissau","parties":["Assembly of the People United"],"education":[],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Nuno Gomes Nabiam"},"Q137049690":{"id":"Q137049690","name":"Horta Inta-A Na Man","desc":"President of Guinea-Bissau since 2025","img":null,"gender":"M","born":"1960-00-00","died":null,"birthplace":null,"parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["military officer"],"wiki":"Horta Inta-A Na Man"}},"summary":"Guinea-Bissau, officially the Republic of Guinea-Bissau, is a country in West Africa that covers 36,125 square kilometres (13,948 sq mi) with an estimated population of 2,080,000. It borders Senegal to its north and Guinea to its southeast.\nGuinea-Bissau was once part of the kingdom of Kaabu, as well as part of the Mali Empire. Parts of this kingdom persisted until the 18th century, while a few others had been under some rule by the Portuguese Empire since the 16th century. In the 19th century, it was colonised as Portuguese Guinea. Upon independence, declared in 1973 and recognised in 1974, the name of its capital, Bissau, was added to the country's name to prevent confusion with Guinea (formerly French Guinea). \nGuinea-Bissau has had a history of political instability: following the bloody but ultimately successful war of independence, the winning PAIGC established a one-party socialist state, which was under military control from 1984 to 1994, when free multiparty elections were held. Several successful military coups have since interrupted peaceful transfer of power, most recently the 2025 coup, which installed the currently ruling military junta, headed by the transitional president Horta Inta-A Na Man.\nAbout 2% of the population speaks Portuguese, the official language, as a first language, and 33% speak it as a second language. Guinea-Bissau Creole, a Portuguese-based creole, is the national language and also considered the language of unity. According to a 2012 study, 54% of the population speak Creole as a first language and about 40% speak it as a second language. The remainder speak a variety of native African languages. The nation is home to numerous followers of Islam, Christianity, and multiple traditional faiths. The country's GDP per capita is one of the lowest in the world.\nGuinea-Bissau is a member of the United Nations, Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, Community of Portuguese Language Countries, Organisation internationale de la Francophonie, Alliance of Small Island States and the South Atlantic Peace and Cooperation Zone. It was also a member of the now-defunct Latin Union. As of November 2025, its membership in Economic Community of West African States and African Union has been suspended after a coup d'etat.","history":"The region now known as Guinea-Bissau, in West Africa, has been inhabited by humans for thousands of years. During the 13th century CE, it was a province of the Mali Empire which later became independent as the empire of Kaabu. The Portuguese Empire claimed the region during the 1450s, but its control was limited to several forts along the coast during most of this period; it gained control of the mainland after a series of \"pacification campaigns\" from 1912 to 1915, which used military forces to violently crush local resistance. The offshore Bijagos Islands were not colonized until 1936. Guinea-Bissau gained independence from Portugal in 1974. The introduction of multi-party politics in 1991 led to the first multi-party elections in 1994. A civil war broke out in 1998, which lasted until the following year.","historyTitle":"History of Guinea-Bissau","curated":null,"indicators":{"gdpPerCapita":[[1970,350.222],[1971,348.277],[1972,388.644],[1973,391.917],[1974,418.643],[1975,435.218],[1976,420.853],[1977,402.907],[1978,402.778],[1979,375.845],[1980,346.855],[1981,477.464],[1982,502.732],[1983,488.994],[1984,407.439],[1985,416.576],[1986,371.138],[1987,487.552],[1988,453.807],[1989,578.491],[1990,651.338],[1991,672.074],[1992,574.321],[1993,581.613],[1994,560.883],[1995,589.487],[1996,614.73],[1997,598.953],[1998,497.724],[1999,478.733],[2000,316.945],[2001,327.138],[2002,362.035],[2003,419.785],[2004,431.377],[2005,463.074],[2006,448.513],[2007,519.07],[2008,640.21],[2009,583.19],[2010,600.194],[2011,719.54],[2012,635.462],[2013,654.305],[2014,652.023],[2015,645.067],[2016,679.665],[2017,783.123],[2018,808.532],[2019,811.21],[2020,846.918],[2021,926.546],[2022,881.603],[2023,964.432],[2024,998.376],[2025,1123.77]],"population":[[1960,577633],[1961,582532],[1962,589474],[1963,594418],[1964,588543],[1965,575005],[1966,566908],[1967,569011],[1968,578170],[1969,581610],[1970,584403],[1971,586221],[1972,586620],[1973,592808],[1974,613338],[1975,650966],[1976,694191],[1977,741789],[1978,791694],[1979,819867],[1980,829303],[1981,842432],[1982,855891],[1983,869592],[1984,883519],[1985,897697],